# T-11234 — the retention floor owned by the sandbox-PROCESS class, separate from the DIRECTORY floor.
#
# WHY A SECOND FLOOR AND NOT A LOWER SHARED ONE (the card's rejected alternative, recorded so it is
# not re-proposed). `cmd_worktree_sweep`'s `--max-age-hours` (default 24, hard-clamped at
# SWEEP_MIN_AGE_HOURS=1) is sized for leaked temp DIRECTORIES, and for a directory that is right: age
# is essentially the ONLY evidence of abandonment there, so a long floor is prudent. Lowering the
# SHARED floor to reach the process class would make directory sweeping more aggressive with no
# evidence calling for that. So the process class gets its OWN floor and the directory floor is
# untouched — same reaper, two axes.
#
# WHY IT MAY BE THIS SHORT. It is opened ONLY by the T-11217 proven-orphan fact: the process's own
# sandbox ROOT is PROVABLY GONE from disk (`_sandbox_root_absent`). A run's root is `mkdtemp`ed at
# verify start and `rmtree`d at the very END of `_run_verify_tests` — after the tests finished and,
# since T-11206/T-11231, after that run's own residents were reaped — so for the entire window in
# which a run has live children its root EXISTS. Root absent therefore establishes orphanhood
# POSITIVELY, at the moment it disappears; waiting another 20 hours buys no safety that is not
# already there. A `-p` process whose root still EXISTS is NOT covered and keeps the full floor,
# because for it age genuinely is the only evidence — exactly the directory case.
#
# WHY 15 MINUTES, derived rather than tuned. Once the root is gone the only residual purpose of a
# floor is the teardown race — the window between the `rmtree` and the run's own children exiting —
# which the own-resident reap immediately above that `rmtree` has already emptied, so it is seconds.
# 15 minutes is two orders of magnitude of slack over that, and it is HALF the 30-minute cron period,
# so a proven orphan is reaped by at latest the SECOND backstop run after it appears (<=45 min)
# rather than after up to 24.5h.
#
# MEASURED, and this is the incident it exists for (controller, this host, 2026-08-17T12:3x): four
# leaked nginx masters alive at 16.0h, 16.1h, 17.3h and 20.1h, each with the sandbox directory named
# in its OWN `-p` argument already gone from disk. A dry-run of the SCHEDULED form skipped all four
# as too young against the 24h default; the same verb at `--max-age-hours 12` reaped all four
# immediately. The cron backstop had been running correctly every 30 minutes throughout — what held
# them was the FLOOR, not the schedule. It is not merely untidy: per T-11231 / the T-11226 incident
# such a leak can inherit a task worktree as its cwd, which makes `worktree adopt` REFUSE, so
# worktree RECOVERY stays blocked for as long as the orphan lives.
SWEEP_ORPHAN_PROC_MIN_AGE_HOURS = 0.25


