class _LandCompatAdmitter:
    """T-11309 (SPEC-0184 rule 1) — the cheap PRE-SELECTION conflict predictor, as a stateful pair.

    THE WALK ALREADY EXISTED; ONLY ITS POSITION WAS WRONG. `_land_merge_members_in_queue_order`
    (rule 3) has always merged members in queue order against an accumulator and removed whoever
    conflicts with a peer merged AHEAD of it. What it could not do is BACK-FILL, because
    `_land_form_batch` had already sliced the queue at `BATCH_MAX` before it ran — so a candidate
    proven unmergeable had by then consumed a capped place and the replacement had been discarded
    upstream. This class is the SAME question asked one step EARLIER, over the SAME oracle
    (`_land_merge_probe`) and the SAME accumulator step (`_land_probe_advance`). It is not a second
    predictor and must never become one (CHARTER §P1 filter 1).

    MEASURED, AND CAUSE-INDEPENDENT. Over the whole journal: 18 `evicted-for-conflict` verdicts across
    7 branches, so 11 of 18 are REPEAT evictions of a branch formation had already failed to merge —
    task/T-11212 seven times, task/T-11098 four, task/T-11291 three, spanning 2026-08-16, -17 and -19.
    Formation read queue ORDER only and carried no memory between rounds, so it re-picked the branch
    it had just failed on and burned a capped slot on it again. Only the five rows of 08-19 share the
    rule-test-ledger cause a sibling card removes; task/T-11212's own documented cause was a
    non-union conflict with `main`. The blindness is therefore a property of the SELECTION, not of any
    one file.

    TWO NAMED ENTRY POINTS, because an implicit one was a real defect (audit-pre finding 1, high,
    2026-08-19). `seed(head)` establishes the accumulator from the slot holder; `admit(branch)`
    judges a peer against it and REFUSES TO ANSWER before a seed. The rejected shape was "the first
    `admit` call is the head" — but the caller admits the head WITHOUT consulting this oracle, so
    that first call would have been PEER 1: the accumulator would have latched onto peer 1 and peer 1
    would have entered the batch UNPROBED against the head. On the AC1 fixture that silently readmits
    the very branch the card exists to skip. Two entry points make the mis-wiring unrepresentable
    rather than merely avoided.

    FAIL-OPEN — THE PREDICTOR'S POLARITY, NOT THE AUTHORITY'S, AND THE DIRECTION IS THE WHOLE POINT.
    A skip is emitted ONLY on a PROVEN blocking set: the oracle's `detail` present AND its
    `unresolved` non-empty, i.e. `_classify_land_merge_conflicts` actually RAN and named a path it
    could not resolve. That is the identical test `_land_prequeue_merge_refusal` applies, and for the
    identical reason. Every answer this cannot prove — an unreadable ref, a git that could not run, an
    unparsable stream, a classifier that raised, a resolved tree that would not compose, a dead
    accumulator — ADMITS, and rule 3 downstream stays the fail-CLOSED authority that removes it.
    So a false admit costs exactly today's eviction, while a false skip would cost capacity — which is
    the very thing this card exists to recover. This is why the polarity here is the OPPOSITE of the
    sibling peer filters (`cost-class`, `supersession-mark`): those answer "is this candidate eligible
    at all" with NO downstream authority behind them, so they must fail closed; this one predicts a
    check that still runs.

    IT IS A PREDICTOR OF RULE 3, NEVER A REPLACEMENT FOR IT. Rule 3 is untouched and still runs on the
    selected set. That is not redundancy: the two differ in polarity (above) and in what they may
    conclude — rule 3 may EVICT with a durable member verdict, this may only SKIP with an
    attempt-scoped sink reason, because a branch that merely lost this round has had nothing proven
    about it that a later round will not re-ask.

    BOUNDED WORK (AC4). At most one probe per candidate EXAMINED, plus one for the seed; `probe_calls`
    records the count. There is no per-round re-probe and no probe on the poll path — this object is
    constructed once per formation, inside `_land_batch_members`, over the snapshot already read."""

    def __init__(self, main_wt, base_rev: "str | None", *, _run_git_cap,
                 _DERIVED_MERGE_ARTIFACTS=None, _dedup_events=None, _probe=None):
        self._main_wt = main_wt
        self._run_git_cap = _run_git_cap
        self._derived = _DERIVED_MERGE_ARTIFACTS
        self._dedup = _dedup_events
        self._probe_in = _probe
        self._accum = base_rev or None
        self._seeded = False
        self.probe_calls = 0

    def _probe(self, accum: str, branch: str) -> "tuple[str, str | None, dict | None]":
        """The SHARED oracle, counted. A 2-tuple injector still works and reads as `detail = None` —
        the correct answer for it, since a seam supplying no classifier verdict has proven nothing,
        and under this class's fail-OPEN polarity an unproven answer ADMITS."""
        self.probe_calls += 1
        if self._probe_in is not None:
            r = self._probe_in(accum, branch)
            return r if len(r) == 3 else (r[0], r[1], None)
        return _land_merge_probe(accum, branch, self._main_wt, _run_git_cap=self._run_git_cap,
                                 _DERIVED_MERGE_ARTIFACTS=self._derived,
                                 _dedup_events=self._dedup)

    def seed(self, head_branch: str) -> None:
        """Establish the accumulator from the SLOT HOLDER, whose membership is not in question.

        A head that does not merge onto `main` leaves the accumulator dead — and that is handled by
        ADMITTING everyone (see `admit`), never by skipping them: a branch-vs-`main` conflict is the
        existing land path's own business and its own abort, exactly as rule 3's `head-unmergeable`
        branch already degrades to a batch of one rather than inventing verdicts it does not own."""
        self._seeded = True
        br = str(head_branch or "").strip()
        if not br or not self._accum:
            self._accum = None
            return
        outcome, tree, _ = self._probe(self._accum, br)
        self._accum = (_land_probe_advance(self._accum, tree, br, self._main_wt,
                                           _run_git_cap=self._run_git_cap)
                       if outcome in _LAND_PROBE_MERGEABLE and tree else None)

    def admit(self, branch: str) -> bool:
        """Would `branch` merge with the members ALREADY selected? Fail-OPEN — see the class docstring.

        REFUSES TO ANSWER BEFORE A SEED, loudly. Returning True here would be the silent readmission
        the audit-pre finding named; returning False would skip a candidate on no evidence at all.
        Neither is a defensible answer to a question that was never set up, so the caller's
        mis-wiring surfaces as a failure instead of as a wrong batch."""
        if not self._seeded:
            raise AssertionError(
                "_LandCompatAdmitter.admit() called before seed() — the accumulator does not yet "
                "hold the head, so this candidate would be judged against the wrong set")
        br = str(branch or "").strip()
        if not br or not self._accum:
            return True                        # nothing proven — the authority downstream decides
        outcome, tree, detail = self._probe(self._accum, br)
        if outcome == "conflict" and detail is not None and list(detail.get("unresolved") or []):
            # PROVEN blocking: the classifier ran and named a path it could not resolve. This is the
            # only branch that skips, and it spends no reservation and no verify slot to reach it.
            return False
        nxt = (_land_probe_advance(self._accum, tree, br, self._main_wt,
                                   _run_git_cap=self._run_git_cap)
               if outcome in _LAND_PROBE_MERGEABLE and tree else None)
        if nxt is not None:
            self._accum = nxt
        # An admitted candidate whose accumulator step FAILED keeps the previous accumulator rather
        # than killing it: the next candidate is then judged against the peers proven so far, which
        # is a weaker but still true statement, and rule 3 re-checks the whole set regardless.
        return True


