# ── SPEC-0184 rules 4 + 7 — ONE verify, ATOMIC fast-forward, red-batch dissolution (T-11194) ──────
# The card that makes the narrowed spec real. Three properties, in the order they matter:
#
#   ATOMICITY (rule 4) — a green batch of N moves `main` by all N in ONE step; a red batch of N
#     leaves `main` byte-identical. Landing a SUBSET is forbidden, because `main` would then hold a
#     combination nothing verified. This is achieved by CONSTRUCTION rather than by a transaction:
#     the surviving peers are merged INTO the candidate worktree before the pass, so the ONE existing
#     `_verify_under_admission` call verifies the COMBINED candidate and the ONE existing in-lock
#     `merge --ff-only <branch>` carries every member in a SINGLE ref move. There is no second ff and
#     no intermediate state a partial application could be observed in — which is a stronger
#     guarantee than a rollback would give, and it adds no new machinery (CHARTER §P1 F1/F2).
#   NO LIVELOCK (rule 4's second half) — a red batch DISSOLVES, and its members are batch-INELIGIBLE
#     for ONE round so the identical batch can never re-form on the next slot. One round, not a
#     quarantine: a member evicted for a PEER's red did nothing wrong.
#   FAIL-CLOSED SPEC-0077 (rule 7) — the pinned-leg trigger is evaluated over the UNION of the
#     members' diffs. If ANY member touches the verify path, the WHOLE batch pays both legs.
#
# WHAT THIS CARD DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO: bisection (T-11195, outside the cut) and any attempt to
# detect a MASKED red — a member red ALONE but green IN COMBINATION.
#   SINCE T-11335, THE FIRST HALF OF THAT SENTENCE IS HISTORY, THE SECOND IS STILL LIVE. Naming the
#   culprit IS built now — `_land_red_isolation_probe` re-runs the failing assertions against each
#   member alone, and a PROVEN non-head culprit is evicted while the remainder takes a FRESH verify
#   (rule 4). T-11195 is closed wont-do, superseded in role. The MASKED-red residual below is
#   UNCHANGED and still accepted: isolation answers "who fails alone", never "who is hiding behind a
#   peer", and the two are different questions. Under batching a member has no
# personal candidate leg, so that residual is structurally invisible here and SPEC-0184 rule 4
# ACCEPTS it rather than pretending to solve it. What is guaranteed is COMBINED-CANDIDATE safety:
# what reaches `main` is EXACTLY what was verified, as a SET.

# The removal reason a peer carries when it merged clean in the T-11193 dry run but not into the
# real candidate. NOT in `_LAND_MEMBER_REMOVAL_EMITS_VERDICT` and that is the substance, not an
# omission (audit-pre finding, absorbed mode-b 2026-08-16): a `conflict` is a durable fact about the
# CHANGES, while this is a fact about THIS ATTEMPT — a ref that moved, a git error. Journaling it as
# a member VERDICT would invite a reader to treat a transient failure as a property of somebody's
# change. The peer keeps its own place in the queue and verifies alone, which is what happens when
# nothing is done to it.
_LAND_MEMBER_CANDIDATE_MERGE_FAILED = batch_landing._LAND_MEMBER_CANDIDATE_MERGE_FAILED  # T-11524 host re-export alias — moved WITH its readers

# T-11220 — the member whose merge would bring NOTHING, because its head is already reachable from the
# candidate. Git's own condition for "Already up to date" is exactly this reachability, and that merge
# exits 0 — which is why an exit-code reading cannot tell "merged your work" from "there was nothing to
# merge". Like `candidate-merge-failed` above and for the same reason, it is NOT in
