def _verify_slot_dir(main_wt: Path) -> Path:
    """The system-temp sidecar dir holding this repo's verify-admission flock slot pool — keyed off
    realpath(main_wt) so every land of the SAME repo shares ONE pool (mirrors `events.journal_lock`'s
    off-tree siding; the slot files themselves are never in the working tree, never committed)."""
    import hashlib
    key = hashlib.sha1(os.path.realpath(str(main_wt)).encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()[:16]
    # T-12185: the PRE-LEVER temp base, NOT the live `tempfile.gettempdir()`. This pool is a
    # CROSS-PROCESS identity — two lands of the SAME repo must resolve the SAME directory, or each
    # believes it holds slot 0 and the SPEC-0132 concurrency bound is silently broken. The
    # `YITC_VERIFY_TMPDIR` lever moves the process temp base, so a land WITH it and a land WITHOUT it
    # would split the pool; asking for the base as it stood BEFORE the lever installed keeps them on
    # one pool. Why that is NARROWER than `events._persistent_lock_root` (T-10755) is single-homed in
    # `events.pre_lever_temp_base`'s own docstring — not restated here.
    d = events.pre_lever_temp_base() / _VERIFY_SLOT_DIRNAME / key
    d.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    return d


# ── SPEC-0184 rule 8 — the superseded-member extinguish (T-11274) ─────────────────────────────
# MEASURED, and it is why this exists: of every batch member whose own land subsequently completed ok,
# 16 of 16 ran a FULL verify anyway (224837ms..541226ms; zero `reverify_skipped`) — so batching's whole
# claimed benefit was, at the member's own land, exactly zero. The head lands the member's content early
# via its fast-forward, but nothing ever told the member so: its own land parks in a flock poll that
# reads the lock and the clock and NOTHING else, then takes a slot and re-verifies content already in
# main. 9 of those 16 were `task/` branches and 7 were `work/` branches — including a bookkeeping-only
# controller-session land that still burned 238880ms — which is why every part of this mechanism is keyed
# BY BRANCH and never by task id.
#
# TWO QUESTIONS, DELIBERATELY SEPARATE — conflating them is the defect this shape avoids.
#   - The MARKER decides WHERE a land stops paying: on the PARK PATH, before it takes the reservation,
#     rather than several steps deeper. It exists for PROMPTNESS, and it must therefore be as cheap to
#     read as the lock the poll already tests — one `exists()`, no journal, no parsing.
#   - The CONTENT check decides WHETHER the land is extinguished AT ALL, and it is the only thing that
#     does. A branch carrying commits not in main is NEVER extinguished, whatever the marker says, and an
#     undecidable answer keeps the land alive.
# So a marker hit is a HINT TO LOOK, never a decision. That is exactly what lets the marker be cheap
# without being trusted: the worst a wrong, stale or forged marker can buy is one wasted git call.


