# T-10853 (SPEC-0077 §3a Diagnostic): the POSITIVE failure-identity discriminators — ONE definition,
# read by BOTH halves of this diagnostic (the excerpt's rescue below AND `bin/yitc-v2`'s
# `_extract_failing_assertion` scans, which import them from here). A second copy of these shapes is
# exactly the P5 drift this single home exists to prevent.
#
# POSITIVE by construction (`lessons/carving-an-exception-into-a-fail-closed-gate.md` §1): each entry
# states a shape that IS a failure identity, so a line survives truncation only by MATCHING one — never
# by failing to match an enumeration of "noise". A noise line nobody anticipated is therefore dropped,
# not kept, which is the safe direction for a diagnostic that must not drown the assertion again.
# The V2 print-runner's own failure line, in BOTH dialects it is written in across this suite:
#   `FAIL: <msg>`          — the collect-and-report `check()` helper (test_t10037 and kin)
#   `FAIL <test_name>: …`  — a file's own `__main__` runner (tests/test_spec0172_cage_contract.py:925)
# T-10946 (the T-10929 deadlock, journal 2026-08-11T06:18:40Z abort_class=rebaseline-unauthorized): only
# the first dialect was recognised, so a file printing the second named ZERO assertions — the excerpt
# rescued none of its FAIL lines out of the elided span AND the extractor returned nothing, so the entry
# surfaced as `(no assertion captured)`. `--rebaseline-waive` then had nothing to bind: both qualified
# tokens the operator declared came back `no-match`, and naming the file is refused BY DESIGN for an
# assertion-bearing failure. A legal re-baseline was unreachable however the authorizer worded it.
# The two branches are DISJOINT (a `:` cannot follow `FAIL` and also be preceded by whitespace), so the
# first is byte-identical to the pre-T-10946 pattern and no input already matched changes answer. The
# name token is `[^\s:]+` — no space, no colon, i.e. an identifier — which keeps this a POSITIVE
# discriminator (`lessons/carving-an-exception-into-a-fail-closed-gate.md` §1) rather than a `^FAIL`
# prefix scan that would swallow prose. The message is OPTIONAL in that branch only: the runner prints
# `FAIL <name>: ` with an empty message when the assertion carries none, and the NAME is already the
# failure identity there — whereas a bare `FAIL:` with nothing after it names nothing, so that branch
# still requires its `\S`.
# T-11416 (X-1052): the banner is recognised WHERE CONSUMERS ACTUALLY PRINT IT. The pattern was
# anchored at column zero with no tolerance for a prefix, and tagging a layer's output is the ordinary
# shell convention — so a whole dialect was invisible to the extractor that exists to read it.
# <project> measured it on their own corpus: 105 aborted lands carrying 133 assertion entries, 96 of
# them (72%) reading `(no assertion captured)`, against 45 emit sites of which 44 carry a bracket tag
# (`[verify]` `[lint]` `[deploy]` `[row-press]` `[drill]` `[canvas-parity]` `[e2e]` `[rollback]`
# `[feature-design]`) and one is indented. The cost is NOT legibility: a `--rebaseline-waive` token
# binds a substring of THAT entry's surfaced assertion, so an entry naming none has nothing to bind —
# the origin of their 61 waive-coverage refusals. They fixed their half by RE-EMITTING each banner
# de-tagged in this matcher's shape, which is precisely the workaround a consumer should not have to
# invent. Three additions, none of which relax the discriminator:
#   - `^\s*` — the one indented emit site (both readers already `.strip()`, so this is belt).
#   - at most ONE leading bracket tag of a NO-SPACE token. Bounded on purpose: a tag is admitted only
#     at line START and cannot contain whitespace, so prose does not acquire a way in, and a log line
#     carrying a bracket elsewhere (alembic's `INFO  [alembic.runtime.migration] Running upgrade`) is
#     as unmatched after this change as before it.
#   - the two separator dialects `FAIL — msg` (hyphen / en- / em-dash) and `FAIL (i) msg` (a short
#     parenthesised index), beside the two already matched. Of the three dialects <project> measured,
#     only `FAIL: msg` matched even UNTAGGED — so the miss was always wider than the tag alone.
# THE BOUND, unchanged and load-bearing: every branch still requires a NON-LETTER separator directly
# after `FAIL`, so `FAILED` / `FAILING` / `FAILURE(S)` cannot be swallowed and rules 0b/1b/2b keep
# owning their own shapes (`_PYTEST_SHORT_SUMMARY_RE`, `_SELF_REPORTED_FAILED_SUMMARY_RE`). Every
# branch remains POSITIVE (`lessons/carving-an-exception-into-a-fail-closed-gate.md` §1) — a line
# survives by MATCHING a stated failure shape, never by failing to match an enumeration of noise.
# Not the inversion `lessons/a-widening-log-means-invert-the-whitelist.md` would suggest for a
# repeatedly-widened recogniser: the inverted form WAS the pre-T-10892 rule-3 fallback (take the last
# non-empty line) and it promoted alembic `INFO` into the assertion position — the same defect
# <project> measured 48 times independently. The structural answer on that axis already shipped as
# T-11346's `_unmarked_layer_failure_id`.
# T-11482 (SPEC-0077 §3a Diagnostic) — the THIRD dialect, and the one that burned this repo's own
# sweep path: a file's `__main__` runner that prints `FAIL <test_name>` with NO colon and nothing
# after it, then KEEPS GOING and prints its remaining `PASS` lines and an `n/m passed` summary
# (`tests/test_t0671_read_order_single_source.py:227`, and its family). Both branches above require
# a colon — branch 1 immediately after `FAIL`, branch 2 after the name — so that line matched
# NEITHER, every scan missed, and the entry surfaced as `(no assertion captured) … last output
# line: 12/13 passed` while the runner had already NAMED the failing test one screen up (live:
# events.jsonl#ts=2026-08-23T12:38:48Z and #ts=2026-08-23T14:36:01Z, abort_class=verify-failed —
# seven aborts across two branches produced no diagnosis, and this blindness was much of why).
# POSITIVE by construction (`lessons/carving-an-exception-into-a-fail-closed-gate.md` §1) and
# bounded on both sides, because this is exactly where a widening turns a fail-closed reader
# fail-open:
#   • END-ANCHORED (`\s*$`) — the line is EXACTLY `FAIL <one-token>`. Prose continuing past the
#     token (`FAIL test_x is broken`) does not match, so this is a SHAPE match, never a `^FAIL`
#     prefix scan.
#   • THE TOKEN IS AN IDENTIFIER, NOT A COUNT (`[A-Za-z_]\w*`) — the bound T-10892 established and
#     T-11377 kept: a marker must name WHICH check failed, never merely THAT something did. A
#     count cannot start with a letter, so `FAIL 3` / `FAIL 12/13` / `FAIL 0` stay outside
#     structurally; so do punctuation- and path-like tails (`FAIL a/b.py`), which no print-runner
#     emits as a test name (audit-pre finding, T-11482).
# DISJOINT from both existing branches — branch 1 needs `:` after `FAIL`, branch 2 needs a `:`
# after the name, this one forbids any `:` in the token and requires end-of-line — so NO input
# that matches today changes answer. Reach: this constant is read by BOTH halves of the
# diagnostic (`_is_verify_failure_line`, which rescues failure lines out of
# `_verify_failure_excerpt`'s elided span, AND rule 0 of `_extract_failing_assertion`), so the
# identity now both SURVIVES truncation and BINDS — no producer change was needed.
# MERGE (T-11416 x T-11482): the two widenings are ORTHOGONAL and both are kept — T-11416 admits the
# leading indent and the single bracket tag plus the dash / parenthesised-index separators, T-11482
# adds the end-anchored colon-less `FAIL <identifier>` branch. Every branch remains POSITIVE, still
# requires a non-letter separator directly after `FAIL`, and the T-11482 branch keeps its own
# end-anchor and identifier-not-count bound verbatim, so neither widening relaxes the other.
_VERIFY_FAIL_TAG_PREFIX = r"(?:\[[^\]\s]{1,32}\]\s*)?"   # at most ONE leading bracket tag
# T-11504 (SPEC-0077 §3a Diagnostic) — the FOURTH dialect, and the one the corpus is actually made
# of: the per-file `check()` idiom `print(("  ok   " if ok else "  FAIL ") + label)` where `label` is
# human PROSE (`tests/test_t11444_segment_aware_readers.py:94`,
# `tests/test_t11446_archive_admission.py:67`, and family). Its rendered line is
# `  FAIL <word> <more words>`, which defeats every branch above: b1/b2 need a colon, b3 needs the
# dash IMMEDIATELY after `FAIL`, b4 needs a no-space parenthesised token, and b5 forbids anything
# past the first token. Measured in this repo on 2026-08-25: 881 literal FAIL print sites across 787
# test files, of which 380 do not match — the prose dialect is the bulk of them. Live cost, the
# T-11501 unfreeze land (events.jsonl#ts=2026-08-24T13:39:42Z, branch
# work/unfreeze-journal-rotation-readers): FIVE pinned failures, FOUR reading `(no assertion
# captured)`, two of them this exact idiom. Driver is the CONSUMER, not this repo — <project> measured
# 72% uncaptured and traced 61 waive-coverage refusals to it (X-1052), where a layer entry keys the
# waive on the captured line itself, so nothing captured means no bindable token at all.
#
# WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY RETIRES, named because it was a stated bound of three shipped cards: "prose
# past the first token disqualifies a banner" (T-10946's `FAIL test_x is broken: prose`, T-11482's
# `FAIL test_x trailing words`, T-11416's `FAIL  AC1 - …`). Owner-directed 2026-08-24 after the
# controller recommended deferring; the three pinned negatives are flipped to positives in the same
# change, assertion-scoped, each with the tie stated inline.
#
# THE THREE BOUNDS THIS BRANCH KEEPS, each still doing work (POSITIVE by construction —
# `lessons/carving-an-exception-into-a-fail-closed-gate.md` §1; this is a SHAPE match anchored at line
# start, never an unbounded `^FAIL` scan):
#   • WHITESPACE directly after `FAIL` — the non-letter-separator rule every branch obeys, so
#     `FAILED` / `FAILING` / `FAILURE(S)` / `FAILS` stay outside and rules 0b/1b/2b keep their inputs.
#   • THE FIRST TOKEN STARTS WITH A LETTER OR UNDERSCORE — T-10892's bound, that a marker names WHICH
#     check failed and never merely THAT one did. `FAIL 3`, `FAIL 12/13`, `FAIL 0` and the count-only
#     `FAIL (3 check(s)):` are excluded structurally, not by enumeration.
#   • A SECOND WHITESPACE-SEPARATED TOKEN IS REQUIRED — T-11482's single-token bounds survive intact:
#     `FAIL a/b.py` (a path is not a runner-emitted name) and `FAIL x!` (a punctuation tail) still do
#     not match. This is also what keeps the branch DISJOINT from b5, which owns the one-token form.
# ON THE WIDENING LOG (`lessons/a-widening-log-means-invert-the-whitelist.md`): this constant now has
# five entries (T-10853, T-10946, T-11416, T-11482, this), so the lesson's tell is live and is
# answered rather than walked past. The inversion here is NOT a card-declared list; it is either the
# pre-T-10892 rule-3 "take the last non-empty line" fallback — TRIED, and it promoted alembic INFO
# into the assertion position 48 measured times — or a stated banner CONTRACT the emitters conform to,
# which is SPEC-0165 item 10's doctrine half and cannot reach ALREADY-SHIPPED output (787 files here
# plus every consumer layer would have to conform first). This branch is the LAST one available along
# the prose axis: the shapes still excluded above are excluded ON PURPOSE, not for want of a branch.
# So if a SIXTH widening of this constant is ever proposed, read the lesson as decisive and take the
# emitter contract instead.
_VERIFY_FAIL_RUNNER_RE = re.compile(
    r"^\s*" + _VERIFY_FAIL_TAG_PREFIX +
    r"FAIL(?::\s*\S|\s+[^\s:]+:|\s*[-\u2013\u2014]\s+\S|\s+\([^()\s]{1,8}\)\s*\S"
    r"|\s+[A-Za-z_]\w*\s*$"
    r"|\s+[A-Za-z_]\S*\s+\S)")
# pytest's short-summary line. The ` - <msg>` half is OPTIONAL (T-10892 / X-0736): under `--tb=no` —
# an ordinary consumer harness setting — pytest prints the summary BARE (`FAILED <nodeid>`), and the
# ` - `-requiring shape matched none of those lines. Every scan then missed, the extractor fell through
# to its last-non-empty-line fallback, and the block named the trailing alembic INFO line while 9 real
# failures sat unnamed in the SAME tail. Still POSITIVE and still anchored: a line must START with the
# `FAILED ` marker and carry a node id.
_PYTEST_SHORT_SUMMARY_RE = re.compile(r"^FAILED\s+\S+(?:\s+-\s+\S.*)?$")
_VERIFY_ERROR_VERDICT_RE = re.compile(r"^(?:[A-Za-z_][\w.]*(?:Error|Exception):|AssertionError)")
# T-11377 (SPEC-0077 §3a Diagnostic): a test that CATCHES its own failures and prints a SELF-REPORTED
# SUMMARY instead of letting an exception escape. The three discriminators above all key off a RAISED
# failure or a pytest/runner line, so such a file's whole output carries no marker any of them match:
# live 2026-08-21T04:37:33Z, batch bat-610872eb3db6, where `tests/test_event_catalog_completeness.py`
# surfaced as `(no assertion captured)` with its own `FAILED (1): AC1 …` sitting in the captured tail,
# and the SPEC-0184 red-isolation oracle correctly declined `entry-names-no-assertion` — the
# attribution machinery worked, its INPUT was unreadable.
#
# WHAT THIS BINDS, and the bound it keeps: a summary that NAMES the failing criteria — the `FAILED`
# verdict marker, then a colon, then a non-empty payload. A COUNT-ONLY summary (`FAILED (3 check(s))`,
# `FAILED — 2 failure(s)`, `2 FAILED`, `3/5 FAILED`) is deliberately NOT matched: it states THAT
# something failed, never WHICH, and promoting it into the assertion position is precisely the
# noise-as-assertion defect T-10892 retired (silence is the honest answer there, and the caller still
# renders the tail's last line as labelled CONTEXT).
#
# POSITIVE by construction (`lessons/carving-an-exception-into-a-fail-closed-gate.md` §1) and anchored
# at line start: the optional prefix is a bare count/ratio (`12 FAILED: …`, `3/5`) or the `CHECK(S)`
# dialect, and `[^:]*` cannot cross a colon — so this is a SHAPE match, never a `FAILED`-anywhere scan
# that would swallow prose (`the block must name the assertion that FAILED: …` does not match, having
# neither the anchor nor the prefix). It overlaps `_PYTEST_SHORT_SUMMARY_RE` on a
# `FAILED <nodeid> - <msg>` line by construction; harmless in both consumers — the extractor reaches
# the pytest rule FIRST (rule 1b), and this tuple is a union.
_SELF_REPORTED_FAILED_SUMMARY_RE = re.compile(r"^(?:\d+(?:/\d+)?\s+)?(?:CHECK\(S\)\s+)?FAILED\b[^:]*:\s*\S")
_VERIFY_FAILURE_MARKER_RES = (_VERIFY_FAIL_RUNNER_RE, _PYTEST_SHORT_SUMMARY_RE, _VERIFY_ERROR_VERDICT_RE,
                              _SELF_REPORTED_FAILED_SUMMARY_RE)

# T-11783 (SPEC-0077 §3a Diagnostic) — the COUNT-ONLY HEADER that OPENS AN ENUMERATED LIST. Several
# of this repo's own test files collect their failures and print, at the very end, a banner stating
# only HOW MANY failed and then one indented `- <criterion>` bullet per failure. Two dialects live
# on 2026-08-28, both from `tests/` here:
#   `FAIL (1 check(s)):`            then `  - AC2: events.jsonl exclusion removed from the audit DIFF`
#                                   (`tests/test_audit_p8_adoption_section.py:145`)
#   `T-11444: 1 check(s) FAILED`    then `  - every surviving migrate site routes through …`
#                                   (`tests/test_t11444_segment_aware_readers.py:1810`)
# Neither banner matches any marker above, CORRECTLY: `_VERIFY_FAIL_RUNNER_RE` needs a colon right
# after `FAIL`, a no-whitespace parenthesised token, or a first token starting with a letter, and
# `(1 check(s))` is none of the three; `_SELF_REPORTED_FAILED_SUMMARY_RE` needs the literal `FAILED`
# at line start, which the second dialect's `T-11444:` prefix denies. So the extractor stopped at a
# header it rightly refused and never read the list one line beneath it, and the failure surfaced as
# `(no assertion captured)` (live: land_completed 2026-08-26T02:41:51Z task/T-11566 and
# 2026-08-25T12:25:09Z task/T-11047, each with the enumerated entry sitting in the labelled CONTEXT).
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO, and the bound is the whole point. The HEADER IS STILL NEVER AN ASSERTION —
# it is not added to any marker tuple, never returned, and a header with NOTHING enumerated beneath
# it still yields SILENCE. Promoting the bare count would be exactly the noise-as-assertion defect
# T-10892 retired and `_SELF_REPORTED_FAILED_SUMMARY_RE` deliberately refuses; this constant only
# OPENS a list, and what is returned is the ENUMERATED ENTRIES. Nor is this the pre-T-10892 "take
# the last non-empty line" fallback: an entry is promoted ONLY while CONTIGUOUSLY beneath a matched
# header, so a bullet anywhere else in the tail is unreachable by this rule — which is why a log
# line (`INFO  [alembic…] Running upgrade`, the 48-times-measured false assertion) cannot arrive
# here however the tail ends.
#
# POSITIVE by construction (`lessons/carving-an-exception-into-a-fail-closed-gate.md` §1) and
# anchored at line start. THE HEADER's bounds:
#   • a COUNT is REQUIRED (`\d+`) and it is the ONLY payload admitted between the verdict word and
#     the check/failure noun — so a banner that NAMES something (`FAIL: AC2 …`) does not match here
#     and stays with the rules that already own it.
#   • the two observed ORDERS only: `FAIL … (<n> check(s)):`-shaped (count then optional colon) and
#     `<prefix>: <n> check(s) FAILED`-shaped (count then the verdict word at end of line).
#   • the noun is `check`/`failure`/`error` (optional `(s)`/`s`) — a bounded vocabulary, not a
#     wildcard, so ordinary prose carrying a number and the word FAIL does not open a list.
# THE ENTRY's bound: a bullet marker (`-`, `*`, `•`) followed by NON-EMPTY text. Both readers of a
# tail `.strip()` their lines, so the ORIGINAL INDENTATION IS ALREADY GONE by the time this matches
# — the bullet, not the indent, is the shape, and the contiguity requirement (enforced at the ONE
# call site, `_extract_failing_assertion` rule 2c) is what keeps it narrow.
_COUNT_ONLY_HEADER_COUNT = r"\(?\s*\d+(?:/\d+)?\s*(?:check|failure|error)(?:\(s\)|s)?\s*\)?"
_COUNT_ONLY_FAILURE_HEADER_RE = re.compile(
    # A — verdict FIRST:  `FAIL (1 check(s)):` / `FAILED (3 check(s))` / `FAILED - 2 failure(s)`
    r"^(?:\S.*?:\s*)?(?:FAIL|FAILED)\s*[-\u2013\u2014]?\s*" + _COUNT_ONLY_HEADER_COUNT + r"\s*:?\s*$"
    # B — verdict LAST:   `T-11444: 1 check(s) FAILED`
    r"|^(?:\S.*?:\s*)?" + _COUNT_ONLY_HEADER_COUNT + r"\s*(?:FAILED|FAIL)\s*$")
_ENUMERATED_FAILURE_ENTRY_RE = re.compile(r"^[-*\u2022]\s+\S")

# T-12175 (SPEC-0077 §3a Diagnostic): the OVER-BUDGET elided-span identity HINT, read back out of the
# marker line `_verify_failure_excerpt` writes in its `elif clipped:` arm (T-11884). That arm holds
# POSITIVE evidence of a named failure — it matched the line and refused it only for LENGTH — and
# prints the line's clipped head as `; first: <head>...`, but INSIDE the `[... ...]` marker so no
# `_VERIFY_FAILURE_MARKER_RES` entry can match it. The bound T-11884 set is deliberate and is NOT
# relaxed: marker recognition still does not widen, and the hint is still not a rescued line. What
# this regex adds is a SECOND, EXPLICIT reader of the producer's own record, so the extractor can
# quote the head the producer already recorded instead of reporting `(no assertion captured)` — a
# claim the producer holds evidence against (measured: T-12165 parked ~3h on a false "order/state
# interaction" diagnosis while `AssertionError: expected 36 concern born blocks, got 37` sat printed
# one line below the mute headline; deviation fingerprint
# pinned-failure-record-elides-assertion-under-budget-misreads-as-no-assertion-captured).
#
# THE SENTENCE KEEPS ONE DEFINITION (CHARTER §P5): the pattern is COMPOSED from
# `_VERIFY_FAIL_OVERBUDGET_MARK` via `re.escape`, never re-typed, so a producer-side reword cannot
# leave a reader matching yesterday's text.
#
# THE HEAD GROUP IS GREEDY, and that is the bound that matters. The producer clips the head to
# `_VERIFY_FAIL_OVERBUDGET_CLIP` chars mid-token, so a head may itself end in — or contain — `...`;
# a non-greedy group would stop at the FIRST such run and silently truncate the very text this rule
# exists to surface. Greedy + the end-anchor takes the longest head the line can offer.
# NO SECOND CLIP AND NO CONTENT RANKING is applied here: the producer already clipped, and deciding
# which line is "the" assertion stays the reader's positional judgement
# (`lessons/fail-closed-belongs-to-the-reader-not-the-parser.md`).
_OVERBUDGET_FIRST_HINT_RE = re.compile(
    r"^\[\.\.\.\s+\d+\s+" + re.escape(_VERIFY_FAIL_OVERBUDGET_MARK)
    + r"\b.*?;\s*first:\s*(?P<head>.+)\.\.\.\s*\.\.\.\]\s*$")

# T-10819: pytest's failure GUTTER. Homed HERE (was `bin/yitc-v2`) as of T-10853 so the excerpt's rescue
# and the extractor normalize identically; `bin/yitc-v2` keeps a thin residue, so its signed
# `#_extract_failing_assertion` anchor and every caller are unmoved. Behaviour byte-identical.
_PYTEST_FAILURE_GUTTER_RE = re.compile(r"^(?:E|>)(?:\s+(?P<rest>.*))?$")


